# Line endings
# https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/git-basics/configuring-git-to-handle-line-endings
#
# Every checkout behaves the same whatever core.autocrlf is set to: text files
# are stored with LF in the repository and checked out with the platform's
# line endings, so a file that was only checked out on Windows never shows as
# modified.
#
# "text=auto" (not plain "text") on purpose: about 280 files inherited from
# upstream were committed with CRLF or mixed line endings. Git leaves files
# that already contain CRLF in the repository untouched under "auto", so they
# do not turn into whitespace-only changes against upstream.
* text=auto

# Shell and Python scripts are also run by bash (Linux, WSL from a Windows
# checkout) and must keep LF in the working tree.
*.sh text=auto eol=lf
*.py text=auto eol=lf

# Windows command scripts keep CRLF in the working tree.
*.cmd text=auto eol=crlf
*.bat text=auto eol=crlf

# Binary files: never converted or diffed as text.
*.bin binary
*.BIN binary
*.fir binary
*.FIR binary
*.mo binary
*.o binary
*.a binary
*.elf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.DNG binary
*.dng binary
*.CR2 binary
*.MLV binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.JPG binary
*.gif binary
*.bmp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.rbf binary
*.lut binary
*.fpm binary
